hedra.","row_title":"Cube → octahedron","col_title":"Dodecahedron {110}","rows":[{"label":"a","detail":"cube"},{"label":"ao","detail":"cube + minor o"},{"label":"co","detail":"cubo-octahedron"},{"label":"oa","detail":"octahedron + minor a"},{"label":"o","detail":"octahedron"}],"cols":[{"label":"0","detail":"no dodecahedron"},{"label":"d1","detail":"minor d{110}"},{"label":"d2","detail":"moderate d{110}"}],"note":"Cubic angles do not depend on the cell edge. Hopper and chevron halite are skeletal and not convex."},"about":"<p>Halite is the textbook cube, but its habit is very sensitive to additives. Formamide, urea and Cd²⁺\nadsorb on {111} and stabilize octahedral faces (Radenovic et al. 2003, 2004, 2005), a habit change explained by\nthe role of water at the surface (van der Voort &amp; Hartman 1990). Skeletal hopper and chevron crystals record salt-pan\nevaporation (Lowenstein &amp; Hardie 1985), and their fluid inclusions preserve brine temperatures (Roberts &amp; Spencer 1995).</p>","habits":[{"name":"Cube","text":"Row a."},{"name":"Octahedron","text":"Additive-induced (row o)."},{"name":"Hopper / chevron (not modelled)","text":"Skeletal, funnel-shaped cubes."}],"references":[{"citation":"Radenovic, N., van Enckevort, W., Verwer, P., Vlieg, E. (2003). Growth and characteristics of the {111} NaCl crystal surface grown from solution. <i>Surface Science</i> 523, 307–315.","doi":"10.1016/S0039-6028(02)02463-9","url":null,"note":"How octahedral {111} NaCl faces form from solution.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"Radenovic, N., van Enckevort, W., Vlieg, E. (2004). Formamide adsorption and habit changes of alkali halide crystals grown from solutions. <i>Journal of Crystal Growth</i> 263, 544–551.","doi":"10.1016/j.jcrysgro.2003.11.103","url":null,"note":"Additive-induced cube-to-octahedron habit change.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"Radenovic, N., van Enckevort, W., Kaminski, D., Heijna, M., Vlieg, E. (2005). Structure of the {111} NaCl crystal surface grown from solution in the presence of CdCl<sub>2</sub>. <i>Surface Science</i> 599, 196–206.","doi":"10.1016/j.susc.2005.10.004","url":null,"note":"Cd²⁺-stabilized octahedral faces.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"van der Voort, E., Hartman, P. (1990). The role of water in the habit change from cube to octahedron of alkali halide crystals. <i>Journal of Crystal Growth</i> 104, 450–456.","doi":"10.1016/0022-0248(90)90146-C","url":null,"note":"Theory of the cube/octahedron habit change.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"Lowenstein, T.K., Hardie, L.A. (1985). Criteria for the recognition of salt-pan evaporites. <i>Sedimentology</i> 32, 627–644.","doi":"10.1111/j.1365-3091.1985.tb00478.x","url":null,"note":"Hopper and chevron halite as depositional indicators.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"Roberts, S.M., Spencer, R.J. (1995). Paleotemperatures preserved in fluid inclusions in halite. <i>Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta</i> 59, 3929–3942.","doi":"10.1016/0016-7037(95)00253-V","url":null,"note":"Fluid inclusions in chevron halite as a paleothermometer.","tags":["thermometry"]}],"cells":[{"id":"a0","row":0,"col":0,"label":"a0","sub":"","title":"a0: cube · no dodecahedron","description":"cube; no dodecahedron.","default":true,"params":{"face_distances":{"100":1.0,"111":null,"110":null},"cap_translation":0.0,"scale":0.5,"planes":{"100":0.5}},"fractions":{"100":1.0},"metrics":{"volume":1.0,"surface_area":6.0,"rsv":0.5,"sphericity":0.806,"aspect":1.6344},"geometry":{"vertices":[[-0.5,-0.5,-0.5],[0.5,-0.5,-0.5],[-0.5,-0.5,0.5],[0.5,-0.5,0.5],[0.5,0.5,-0.5],[-0.5,0.5,-0.5],[0.5,0.5,0.5],[-0.5,0.5,0.5]],"faces":[{"form":"100","hkl":[1,0,0],"loop":[3,1,4,6]},{"form":"100","hkl":[0,1,0],"loop":[6,4,5,7]},{"form":"100","hkl":[-1,0,0],"loop":[5,0,2,7]},{"form":"100","hkl":[0,0,1],"loop":[7,2,3,6]},{"form":"100","hkl":[0,-1,0],"loop":[2,0,1,3]},{"form":"100","hkl":[0,0,-1],"loop":[1,0,5,4]}]},"files":{"stl":"models/stl/halite/halite_a0.stl","step":"models/step/halite/halite